we're going to refactor out any output transformation that deals with anything except the graph edges - do that first

then to display the information about the commit we'll use a jj log template that outputs the information about the commit in json and use that to echo the information about each commit

we'll use a templating system with some defined "functions"

newlines in this string will be spaces, and a character literal of `\n` will denote a new line - so the template a few lines below would be a single line of output

anything in %{} is a "function" call in our DSL, see functions below

'''
%{elastic_tab(change_id(len=6))}
%{elastic_tab(commit_id(len=6))}
%{elastic_tab(author_initials(mine="ME"))}
%{elastic_tab(timestamp("YYMMDD·HHMM"))}
%{working_copies()}
%{bookmarks()}
%{tags()}
%{description_title()}
'''

elastic_tab(...) aligns the left side of its context as per current columnar alignment behaviour for eg. the change id. Any space greater than 1 will use the current behaviour of dashes including the dash start and end chars

commit_id(len=8) shows the first len chars of the commit id in bright black, with the commit_id_shortest prefix in blue

change_id(len=8, show_divergence=true) shows the first len characters of the change_idd with the change_id_shortest in magenta. It also shows divergence. If change_offset is non-zero then it should add `/<change_offset>` after the change id. This can cause the overall output to be longer than len.

author_initials(mine="") outputs the first and last letters of the author. If `mine` is a string and mine = true in the input, then instead of echoing the author initials, echo the passed in value of `mine`.

timestamp(format) takes a standard date format to render the timestamp

working_copies() shows all of the working copies for this revision in green

bookmarks() the same for bookmarks, and tags() for tags, both in magenta

description_title() displays the first line of the description

the returned output will have some graph nodes and/or edges, then some whitespace before the JSON payload

